'''Okayama Prefecture''' ({{nihongo|丘山県|Okayama-ken}}) is a prefecture of [[Okaiken]] located in the [[Sanzu Region]] on the island of [[Sanzu]] (三州), the fourth-largest island in the archipelago. Its population as of 2026 was estimated at 1,743,736. It is the 24th most populous prefecture and the 24th largest by land area, spanning approximately 4,850.56 square kilometres. Its capital city is [[Anjō-Saikawa]].

Okayama Prefecture occupies the eastern half of Sanzu Island, a peninsula jutting into the Shendan Ocean with coastline on three sides — east, south, and west. This maritime exposure has shaped the prefecture's identity as Okaiken's citrus capital and one of its most important shipbuilding centers. The prefecture is the nation's largest producer of [[mikan]] (mandarin oranges) and [[iyokan]] citrus, and its shipyards on the eastern coast are among the most productive in Sanzu. Okayama is often called "The Citrus Forge" (柑橘の炉, ''Kankitsu no Ro'') — a name that captures the fusion of agricultural abundance and industrial might that defines the prefecture.

==History==
[[File:Okayama Citrus Heritage.jpg|thumb|left|250px|Traditional citrus drying racks on the coast of Sanzu Island, a practice dating back to the early Sengoku period.]]

===The Citrus Lords and the Sanzu Confederation===
The eastern half of Sanzu Island has been a center of citrus cultivation since the medieval period. The warm ocean currents that wrap around the peninsula create a subtropical microclimate ideally suited to citrus, and by the 14th century, the coastal villages of the region were already producing mikan and yuzu for export to the Harujima mainland. The citrus trade enriched a class of maritime lords known as the [[Citrus Lords]] (柑橘大名, ''Kankitsu-daimyō''), who controlled the orchard territories and the shipping routes that carried the fruit northward.

During the Sengoku period, the three prefectures of Sanzu — Okayama, Shiogami, and Naruto — formed the [[Sanzu Confederation]] (三州連合, ''Sanzu-rengō''), a mutual defense pact against the expansionist ambitions of the Honda Shogunate on the Harujima mainland. The Confederation maintained a formidable naval force, and the shipyards of the eastern Sanzu coast — the ancestors of today's Okayama shipbuilding industry — produced the warships that kept the Shogunate at bay for over a century.

===Integration and the citrus boom===
The Saito Restoration of 1871 brought Sanzu Island under central government control. On June 22, 1872, the Imperial government formally established Okayama Prefecture, naming it after the historic Okayama-no-miya (丘山宮), the "Palace of the Hill and Mountain," the fortified residence of the most powerful Citrus Lord. The capital was established at [[Anjō-Saikawa]], a coastal town at the narrow waist of the peninsula that commanded both the eastern and western shipping lanes.

The late 19th and early 20th centuries saw a massive expansion of the citrus industry. New cultivars — including the prized [[Okayama mikan]] and the [[iyokan]] — were developed through selective breeding programs, and the completion of the [[Harujima Shinkansen]] extension to Okayama in 1982 connected the prefecture to the national high-speed rail network, enabling daily shipment of fresh citrus to the metropolitan markets of [[Okami]] and [[Toyozawa]].

===Modern era===
In recent decades, Okayama has leveraged its maritime position to become a major shipbuilding center, complementing its traditional agricultural base with heavy industry. The [[Sanzu Strait]] between the island and the Harujima mainland is one of the busiest shipping lanes in Okaiken, and the eastern coast of Okayama — with its deep natural harbors and proximity to the open Shendan Ocean — has become the preferred site for commercial shipyards serving the national and international market.

==Geography==
[[File:Sanzu Peninsula.jpg|thumb|right|300px|Aerial view of the Sanzu peninsula, showing Okayama's three-sided coastline and the citrus orchards that blanket the coastal hills.]]

Okayama Prefecture occupies the eastern half of [[Sanzu Island]] (三州), the fourth-largest island in the Okaiken archipelago. Sanzu Island sits between [[Harujima]] to the north and the [[Yagikoshu]] mainland to the south, with the [[Sanzu Strait]] separating it from Harujima and the [[Sanzu South Channel]] separating it from Yagikoshu. The prefecture spans approximately 4,850.56 km², making it the 24th largest in Okaiken. The defining geographic feature is the peninsula itself — a roughly triangular landmass with coastline on three sides: east, south, and west.

The eastern coast faces the [[Sanzu Inland Sea]] (三州内海, ''Sanzu-naiumi''), the sheltered body of water between Sanzu Island and the [[Shiogami]] island chain to the east, beyond which lies the open [[Katsuran Sea]]. The deep-water harbors along this coast support the shipbuilding industry. The [[Ishida]] and [[Kaiyo]] districts on the southeastern coast operate the largest commercial ports, handling both ship construction and cargo traffic. The southern coast, from [[Kamiita]] to [[Nakayama]], faces the [[Sanzu South Channel]] toward Yagikoshu and is characterized by rocky headlands, sheltered coves, and the terraced citrus orchards that climb the coastal hills. The southwestern coast, including the [[Nogaya]] district at the peninsula's tip, looks out over the waters between Sanzu and Yagikoshu, and operates smaller fishing harbors and ferry terminals.

The interior of the peninsula is mountainous, with the [[Okayama Mountain Range]] (丘山脈, ''Okayama-sanmyaku'') running north-south through the center. The mountains rise to approximately 1,200 metres and are heavily forested with [[sugi]] cedar and [[hinoki]] cypress. The [[Tsurugi]] district in the deep south is the most mountainous and least populated area, with steep valleys and dense primary forest.

