==Economy==
[[File:Okayama Shipyard.jpg|thumb|right|300px|The Okayama Shipbuilding Complex on the eastern coast, one of the largest commercial shipyards in Sanzu.]]

Okayama Prefecture's economy is built on two dominant pillars: [[citrus agriculture]] and [[shipbuilding]]. The prefecture's three-sided coastline and subtropical climate make it Okaiken's most productive citrus-growing region, while its deep-water harbors on the eastern coast support one of the nation's most important shipbuilding clusters. These two industries — one rooted in the soil, the other in the sea — define the prefecture's identity and account for the majority of its economic output.

===Citrus agriculture===
[[File:Okayama Mikan Harvest.jpg|thumb|left|250px|Mikan harvest on the terraced hillsides of the southern coast, the heart of Okayama's citrus industry.]]

Okayama Prefecture is the largest citrus-producing region in Okaiken, accounting for approximately 40% of national output. The prefecture's frost-free growing season, abundant sunshine, and mineral-rich volcanic soils produce fruit of exceptional quality, and the Okayama name has become synonymous with premium citrus in the domestic market.

The flagship product is the [[Okayama mikan]] (丘山みかん), a small, seedless mandarin orange with a loose, easy-to-peel skin and a characteristically sweet, slightly tart flavor. The Okayama mikan is cultivated across the southern and western coastal districts, where the terraced orchards climbing the hillsides above the ocean are one of the most iconic landscapes in Sanzu. The [[Sakurai]] and [[Kitaoka]] districts are the primary growing areas, and the harvest season — from November through January — is a major economic and cultural event.

The prefecture also produces the [[iyokan]] (伊予柑), a larger, more intensely flavored citrus that is prized for its thick, fragrant peel and juicy segments. The iyokan, which ripens later than the mikan, extends the citrus season through March and has become an increasingly important export product. Other significant citrus varieties include the [[dekopon]] (a premium hybrid mandarin), the [[kawachi-bankan]] (a large, grapefruit-like citrus unique to the Sanzu region), and the [[yuzu]], which is cultivated in the cooler mountainous districts.

The [[Okayama Citrus Cooperative]] is one of the largest agricultural cooperatives in Okaiken, representing over 15,000 member farms and managing the branding, quality certification, and distribution of Okayama citrus products. The cooperative operates the [[Okayama Citrus Terminal]] in Anjō-Saikawa, a state-of-the-art sorting and cold-storage facility that processes over 200,000 tonnes of citrus annually for shipment via the Shinkansen and coastal freighter.

Processed citrus products are a growing sector. The prefecture produces [[mikan juice]], [[citrus vinegar]], [[marmalade]], [[citrus-infused shochu]], and [[dried mikan peel]] for use in traditional medicine and cooking. The [[Kamiita]] district on the southern coast is the center of the processing industry, operating several large-scale facilities that produce both consumer products and industrial citrus extracts.

===Shipbuilding===
[[File:Okayama Ship Launch.jpg|thumb|right|300px|A container ship launch at the Ishida shipyard, a dramatic ceremony that draws thousands of spectators.]]

Okayama Prefecture is one of Okaiken's three major shipbuilding centers — alongside [[Naruto Prefecture]] and [[Aizawa Prefecture]] — and the largest on the Sanzu island chain. The industry is concentrated on the eastern coast along the [[Sanzu Inland Sea]], where the deep natural harbors of the [[Ishida]] and [[Kaiyo]] districts provide ideal conditions for large-scale ship construction. The [[Okayama Shipbuilding Complex]], a cluster of interconnected yards and dry docks stretching along 12 kilometres of coastline, is one of the largest facilities of its kind in the nation.

The shipyards produce a full range of commercial vessels, including container ships, bulk carriers, automobile carriers, and liquefied natural gas tankers. The complex also builds [[naval vessels]] under contract with the [[Sanese Imperial Forces]], including destroyers and patrol craft for the [[Sanese Imperial Navy]]. The industry employs approximately 18,000 workers directly and supports an additional 45,000 jobs in related sectors — steel fabrication, marine engineering, electronics, and port logistics.

The [[Ishida Shipyard]] is the flagship facility, capable of constructing vessels up to 200,000 deadweight tonnes. The shipyard's launch ceremonies — in which a completed vessel slides down a greased slipway into the Sanzu Inland Sea amid fireworks and crowds of spectators — are major public events that draw thousands of residents and have become a cultural tradition unique to the eastern coast.

In recent years, the shipyards have expanded into [[offshore wind platform]] construction, building the massive floating foundations for wind turbines deployed in the Shendan Ocean. The [[Okayama Offshore Energy Initiative]], a public-private partnership between the prefectural government and the major shipyards, aims to position the eastern coast as the national center for renewable energy maritime infrastructure.

===Fishing and aquaculture===
The three-sided coastline supports a diverse fishing industry, with different species targeted on each coast. The eastern coast, facing the sheltered Sanzu Inland Sea, supports a fleet targeting [[sea bream]], [[horse mackerel]], and [[nori]] seaweed cultivation. The southern coast, facing the Sanzu South Channel toward Yagikoshu, produces [[abalone]], [[sea urchin]], and [[amberjack]]. The southwestern coast around Nogaya supports [[mackerel]], [[sardine]], and [[pearl farming]].

The [[Nogaya]] district at the southwestern tip operates a significant [[pearl farming]] industry, cultivating Akoya pearls in the calm, nutrient-rich waters between Sanzu and Yagikoshu. The [[Mugi]] district on the western coast is the center of the nori industry, producing some of the highest-grade seaweed in Sanzu.

===Other industries===
The mountainous interior supports [[forestry]], with the [[Tsurugi]] district producing high-quality cedar and cypress timber. The [[Kamiyama]] district operates a growing [[wood pulp]] and [[paper]] industry. The prefecture also produces [[sudachi]] and [[yuzu]] in the cooler mountain districts, complementing the lowland citrus industry.
In the wider regional economy of Sanzu, Okayama operates as the heavy industrial engine and primary ro-ro ferry freight hub connecting the Inland Sea with the outer island shipping corridors. The Okayama Shipbuilding Complex has expanded beyond bulk carriers to construct high-efficiency hybrid-electric passenger ferries, specialized cattle carriers, and refrigerated coastal container ships. The prefecture's marine engineering plants supply low-emission diesel and hydrogen propulsion engines to yards throughout Sanzu, while its coastal processing facilities bottle yuzu juices and sweet sake marinades for export to metropolitan Harujima.

==Transportation==
[[File:Okayama Shinkansen.jpg|thumb|left|250px|The Harujima Shinkansen crossing the Sanzu Strait Bridge, connecting Okayama to Harujima.]]

Okayama Prefecture is connected to the national rail network by the [[Harujima Shinkansen]], which crosses to [[Harujima]] via the [[Sanzu Strait Bridge]] — a 3.8-kilometre suspension bridge completed in 1982 that carries both road and rail traffic across the strait separating Sanzu from the northern mainland. The Shinkansen makes three stops within the prefecture: [[Sato Station]], [[Anjō-Saikawa Station]] (the capital), and [[Ishida Station]] on the eastern coast.

The [[Okayama Expressway]] runs north-south through the center of the peninsula, connecting the capital to the northern coast along the Sanzu Strait and the southern coast facing Yagikoshu. The [[Nogaya Ferry Terminal]] on the southwestern coast operates regular services to the Shiogami and Naruto prefectures across the Sanzu Inland Sea, while the [[Ishida Port]] handles deep-water cargo and the occasional international call.

The [[Okayama-Sanzu Airport]] near Anjō-Saikawa offers domestic flights to [[Okami]], [[Toyozawa]], and [[Aizawa]].

==Culture==
[[File:Okayama Citrus Festival.jpg|thumb|right|300px|The Okayama Citrus Festival, the largest citrus celebration in Okaiken.]]

Okayama's culture is defined by citrus and the sea. The prefecture's most important cultural event is the [[Okayama Citrus Festival]] (丘山柑橘祭り, ''Okayama-kankitsu Matsuri''), held each December in Anjō-Saikawa during the peak mikan harvest. The festival features citrus-tasting competitions, mikan-picking events, a citrus-themed parade with floats shaped like giant mikan, and the popular "Citrus Queen" pageant. The event attracts over 120,000 visitors annually and is one of the largest food festivals in Sanzu.

The [[Ishida Ship Launch Festival]] (石田進水祭, ''Ishida-shinsui-sai'') celebrates each major vessel launch from the Ishida shipyard with fireworks, taiko drumming, and a ceremonial blessing by a Shinto priest. The launches, which occur approximately six to eight times per year, have become a cultural tradition that brings the entire community together.

The [[Nogaya Pearl Festival]] (野谷真珠祭り, ''Nogaya-shinju Matsuri'') on the southwestern coast celebrates the prefecture's pearl farming heritage with pearl-diving demonstrations, jewelry exhibitions, and a market selling locally cultured pearls at below-retail prices.

The [[Sanzu Strait]] between Sanzu and Harujima to the north is known for its dramatic tidal currents, and the [[Sanzu Strait Current Festival]] in Mugi features traditional boat races that pit crews against the powerful tidal flow — a sporting tradition that dates back to the Sengoku period.

==Education==
Okayama Prefecture is home to the [[Okayama University of Marine Technology]], a research institution specializing in naval architecture, marine engineering, and oceanographic research. The university operates a model basin for hull testing and maintains partnerships with the major shipyards for applied research. The [[Okayama Citrus Research Institute]], affiliated with the [[Okami Imperial University]] system, is the nation's leading center for citrus breeding, pest resistance, and post-harvest technology.

